sections.s2_technical:
A `ReentrantReadWriteLock` exposes two sub-locks: `readLock()` which many threads can hold at once, and `writeLock()` which only one thread can hold and which excludes all readers. The lock is reentrant, so the thread holding the write lock can also acquire the read lock.

Downgrading works because the write lock is exclusive. While you hold the write lock, no other thread holds any lock, so you can acquire the read lock (which succeeds immediately since you are the only holder), and then release the write lock. At that moment you still hold the read lock, so your freshly written value cannot be changed by another writer before you read it, and other readers may now join you.

```java
ReentrantReadWriteLock rw = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
rw.writeLock().lock();                 // exclusive
try {
    rate = newRate;                    // WRITE
    rw.readLock().lock();              // acquire read BEFORE releasing write (downgrade)
} finally {
    rw.writeLock().unlock();           // now only read held — downgrade complete
}
try {
    return applyRate(rate);            // READ the fresh value safely
} finally {
    rw.readLock().unlock();
}
```

Upgrading is forbidden by design. If a thread holds the read lock and calls `writeLock().lock()`, it blocks because the write lock needs exclusivity and there is at least one reader (itself) still holding the read lock, and it will not release its own read lock, so it waits on itself. Worse, if two threads both hold the read lock and both attempt to upgrade, each waits for the other to drop its read lock, which neither will, giving a classic deadlock. The ReentrantReadWriteLock documentation explicitly states upgrade is not supported, and the call simply blocks forever rather than succeeding.

```java
rw.readLock().lock();
try {
    rw.writeLock().lock();   // DEADLOCK RISK: blocks, never acquires
} finally { /* ... */ }
```

The correct workaround when you discover you need to write after reading is to release the read lock, acquire the write lock, and re-check the condition because the state may have changed in the gap — the standard check-then-act-under-write pattern.

A senior nuance: downgrading is valuable precisely because it closes the race window. Without it, you would release the write lock and then separately acquire the read lock, and in that tiny gap another writer could change the value, so the value you read would not be the one you wrote. Downgrading guarantees continuity of protection.

